.page wait 1 wait for jobs and processes to finish

# SYNOPSIS
*wait* [_PID_ | _JOB_]...

# DESCRIPTION
`wait` is a gapsh builtin. With operands it waits for each process _PID_ or job _JOB_ (`%1`, `%%`, `%cat`, see jobs(1)) to finish and returns the status of the last one: its exit status, or 128 plus the signal that killed it. A job's status is that of its last process. Without operands it waits until no job runs any more and returns 0.

`wait` waits for jobs to end, not to stop: a job that stops meanwhile is still waited for, and one that is already stopped (the shell says so) only ends after `fg` or `bg`. In an interactive shell ^C stops the waiting and `wait` returns 130.

# EXIT STATUS
The status of the last operand; 127 if it is not a child of the shell or no such job exists; 130 after ^C.

# EXAMPLES
    sleep 2 & wait $!; echo done
    (sleep 1; exit 7) & wait %1; echo $?

# SEE ALSO
jobs(1), kill(1)
